  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On May 10–11, Iran delivered its formal response to the US framework via Pakistan, and Trump within hours called it "a piece of garbage" and declared the ceasefire "on massive life support." By May 12, Pakistan's diplomacy was described as scrambling to prevent collapse. This exchange constitutes the sharpest deterioration since the ceasefire was brokered: the US-authored 14-point MOU framework has effectively been countered by Iran's own 14-point plan (war-end terms without nuclear provisions), and Trump's public rejection removes any ambiguity about the US reception. The gap between the two positions — Iran demanding reparations, Hormuz sovereignty, and no nuclear talks; the US demanding nuclear constraints and a 30-day framework — widened publicly, not narrowed. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span> (0.35→0.14), <span class="cite">F6</span> (0.45→0.18), supports <span class="cite">N1</span> creation</p>
      <p>The question of whether any named Iranian official publicly addressed the US-authored 14-point MOU framework before EOD May 13 now has a definitive answer: the available evidence does not support YES. Baghaei's weekly presser statements defended Iran's own 14-point counterproposal; Ghalibaf's May 11–12 X posts — while explicitly naming Iran's "14-point proposal" — came from a parliament speaker not on the resolution-criteria list (Pezeshkian, Araghchi, Baghaei, Larijani, Mojtaba Khamenei) and addressed Iran's outgoing document rather than the US-authored framework. Araghchi's departure to BRICS India without a press-on-record statement about the MOU closes the F1 window. Per the ambiguity rule, broad negotiation commentary without specific MOU framework citation resolves as NO. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span> (RESOLVE-NO)</p>
      <p>On the Lebanon front, IDF military tempo on May 12–13 accelerated in southern Lebanon without entering Beirut governorate. The Kfardounin strike (May 12, Nabatieh district) killed six; May 13 saw 13+ strikes in Nabatieh and IDF evacuation warnings for seven villages. The Jiyeh highway strike (May 13) hit the Mount Lebanon governorate border zone — the ambiguity rule requires explicit "Beirut strike" designation by IDF or Lebanese government, which has not been issued. Hezbollah fired rockets at IDF troops in south Lebanon on May 13 without reaching the &gt;20/day threshold. Washington talks between Israel and Lebanon are scheduled, which historically correlates with pre-talks IDF pressure operations. The overall operational pattern puts the probability of a Beirut-governorate strike within the remaining 2-day window at an elevated level, but no confirmed intra-governorate strike has yet occurred. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span> (0.30→0.45)</p>
      <p>The Saudi airspace question is empirically murkier than originally modeled. The factual record through May 13 shows: a second Trump-MBS call (positive indicator, now OBSERVED), WSJ reporting of lifted restrictions (partial positive), and a Saudi deputy minister denial of authorization for "offensive operations" (complication for resolution criteria). Saudi Arabia appears to have restored logistics-level basing access while publicly maintaining a position against offensive-strike airspace use — a distinction the forecast's resolution criteria cannot cleanly resolve without a joint Pentagon-MOFA public statement, which has not been issued. Saudi Arabia's continued support for Pakistan's mediation role and its pro-de-escalation public messaging run against issuing a statement that would be read as endorsing US military posture against Iran. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span> (0.40→0.35)</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
