      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (38%):</strong> F4 at 45% and N1 at 30% are the primary escalation drivers — F4 covers Lebanon-front kinetic escalation while N1 covers full US-Iran track collapse; F3 (formal rejection, 15%) is a tertiary path that remains unlikely but non-trivial.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (47%):</strong> The main scenario if neither the Pakistan channel formally collapses (N1 stays below YES) nor a de-escalation pathway opens; Iran's PGSA toll equilibrium and IRGC financial stake in partial Hormuz closure point toward sustained low-intensity stalemate as the most likely single outcome.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (15%):</strong> F2 at 14% is the primary de-escalation anchor; F5 (35%) and F6 (18%) are downstream indicators well below threshold — the Pakistan channel's continued existence prevents the probability floor falling below 15%.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>The Iranian leadership's behavior through May 13 shows a fractured-but-functional decision structure. Pezeshkian's May 10 X post ("not surrender, but defense of national interests") and Baghaei's May 11 presser defense of Iran's own 14-point plan as "reasonable and generous" together constitute the public communications frame: Iran is not retreating, it submitted a fair offer, and the failure is Washington's. This is not rejection language; it is the Iranian diplomatic default of positioning the other party as the obstructionist. The IRGC channel has not issued an endorsement of the diplomatic track (<span class="cite">F2</span> indicator remains NOT_OBSERVED), and Ghalibaf's aggressive tone on May 11–12 ("armed forces ready to shock aggressors") coexists with his endorsement of the 14-point diplomatic plan — a dual messaging consistent with IRGC-adjacent political figures who are not blocking the channel but are not investing in it either. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>
      <p>Araghchi's departure for BRICS India on May 12 for a May 14–15 summit carries dual significance. The trip is a multilateral legitimacy operation — Iran using BRICS to demonstrate that it is not diplomatically isolated despite the US-Israel war — but it physically removes the lead negotiator from the Pakistan channel during a critical window. The Iran International reporting from April 30 about Pezeshkian and Ghalibaf seeking Araghchi's ouster over alleged IRGC subservience adds a bureaucratic-friction layer: Araghchi's negotiating position may not fully reflect either presidential or IRGC preferences, creating an internal coherence problem for any deal text. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Iran's de facto two-track Hormuz management — PGSA toll collection alongside periodic IRGC interdiction — has produced a behavioral outcome as of May 12 that is more durable than initially expected: 18 vessels on May 11 represent not recovery but a stabilized minimal-throughput regime that generates IRGC revenue (Newsmax May 12 reports companies quietly paying) while keeping Western insurers out and blocking any Lloyd's premium reduction that would signal genuine reopening. This toll-extraction equilibrium gives the IRGC a financial stake in the status quo that runs directly counter to an MOU-based diplomatic resolution. The US Treasury OFAC warning against toll payments creates a sanction-compliance gap that Western majors cannot bridge — Maersk/MSC/Frontline return announcements remain at zero. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

